WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.690
In recent times the meetings between the US
President and European leaders have become

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:07.090
quite tense.

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:17.360
The NATO summit, which took place in early
July 2018 has been good proof of that... and

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.360
has left some questions on the table:

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.699
Is Donald Trump right when criticizing his
European partners?

00:00:22.699 --> 00:00:27.020
Are the European NATO countries taking advantage
of the United States Army?

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:35.370
And perhaps most importantly: Should Europe
have an army?

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:40.390
Listen up.

00:00:40.390 --> 00:00:44.239
If one thing has become very clear, it’s
that Donald Trump isn’t too fond of these

00:00:44.239 --> 00:00:45.720
European leader meetings...

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:46.720
[Simon’s Ironic Mode ON]

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:50.500
How bothersome it is to have to meet with
all these people...

00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:57.350
Poor Trump, if they were at least as fun as
Vladimir Putin or Kim Jong Un... it’d be

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:58.350
different.

00:00:58.350 --> 00:01:01.339
[Simon’s Ironic Mode OFF]

00:01:01.339 --> 00:01:09.009
The fact, dear viewers, is that the clash
between the US Administration and most European

00:01:09.009 --> 00:01:26.259
governments extends to virtually all issues:
economy, trade, immigration...

00:01:26.259 --> 00:01:28.079
and defense.

00:01:28.079 --> 00:01:33.649
See, the US president has been criticizing
NATO for years due to the alleged lack of

00:01:33.649 --> 00:01:35.529
involvement of the European partners.

00:01:35.529 --> 00:01:42.169
In fact, the US president has been saying
things like that for almost 20 years.

00:01:42.169 --> 00:01:44.969
(“America has no vital interest” in Europe.

00:01:44.969 --> 00:01:47.739
Their conflicts are not worth American lives.

00:01:47.739 --> 00:01:52.490
Pulling back from Europe would save this country
millions of dollars annually”.

00:01:52.490 --> 00:01:53.549
Donald Trump, 2000.)

00:01:53.549 --> 00:01:57.489
Of course, it isn’t just about the White
House tenant.

00:01:57.489 --> 00:02:01.950
The truth is that in recent decades the differences
between Europe and America have expanded.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:07.239
For example, there’s baseball versus soccer,
an appreciation for cars with gigantic engines

00:02:07.239 --> 00:02:12.180
- one can tell where gas is much cheaper - and,
also, the way in which weapons are considered...

00:02:12.180 --> 00:02:14.659
in all fields... even in defense.

00:02:14.659 --> 00:02:19.250
Yes, the army and the military are very, very
important issues in US politics...

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:23.590
However in Europe... not so much... well,
it’s even worse than not so much.

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:28.860
These aren’t even front page issues and
are certainly not very popular.

00:02:28.860 --> 00:02:33.109
Perhaps that’s why, since the cold war ended,
the military spending of European countries

00:02:33.109 --> 00:02:38.790
that belong to NATO has collapsed in terms
of GDP; ranging from something a little over

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:40.910
3% to less than 1.5%.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:43.560
In Europe, military spending doesn’t sell.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:47.709
And this, dear viewers, has greatly angered
Washington’s politicians in recent years.

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:51.500
And, in this matter, we’re not only talking
about Donald Trump, but also Barack Obama

00:02:51.500 --> 00:02:54.170
and George W. Bush.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:58.989
Here on VISUALPOLITIK, in a previous video,
we told you why it isn’t such a good idea

00:02:58.989 --> 00:03:02.450
for European countries to spend more money
on weapons

00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:04.220
But... just a moment...

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:06.349
What if Washington was right?

00:03:06.349 --> 00:03:10.459
Listen up.

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:16.879
(WHAT IF WASHINGTON WAS RIGHT)

00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:24.420
Here’s a question and as always you can’t
Google it: Do you know how many soldiers the

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:27.709
European NATO countries have in total?

00:03:27.709 --> 00:03:30.430
Well, approximately 1.8 million soldiers.

00:03:30.430 --> 00:03:36.299
However, of this number, only 30% can be deployed
in case of a conflict... and only 50 to 100

00:03:36.299 --> 00:03:40.780
thousand can be deployed sustainably and for
greater lengths of time.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:47.030
And folks, in general, the European armies
have many operational problems.

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:51.819
Yes, it's true, they spend a lot of money...
but their governments, most of them, don’t

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:53.540
really care much about the result.

00:03:53.540 --> 00:03:58.340
We can, for example, take a look at Germany’s
case, a country that in 2017 had a military

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:01.529
budget of approximately 45 billion dollars.

00:04:01.529 --> 00:04:07.069
That is, it has one of the top 10 largest
military budgets of any country.

00:04:07.069 --> 00:04:12.920
Well, despite being one of the countries with
the largest military budget, a couple of years

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:18.720
ago a study by the consultancy McKinsey revealed
that almost 60% of Germany’s Eurofighter

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:24.090
and Tornado fighter aircrafts and 80% of their
most modern helicopters, weren’t ready for

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:29.180
combat, due to a lack of maintenance, updating,
supplies, etc., etc.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:35.590
At the end of 2017 a parliamentary investigation
showed quite similar results: not one of the

00:04:35.590 --> 00:04:44.860
submarines and none of the 14 main transport
planes of the German air force were operational.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:45.860
Not even one.

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:46.860
You heard that right.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.780
Even a document from the German Ministry of
Defense warned that the soldiers didn’t

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:55.300
have enough protective vests, winter clothes
or tents to participate in any important NATO

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.310
mission.

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:00.650
And it's not just Germany...

00:05:00.650 --> 00:05:05.900
For example, during the war in Libya, despite
being a relatively small conflict, just 3

00:05:05.900 --> 00:05:13.500
weeks after the attacks began the European
air forces began to run out of precision weapons.

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:18.330
In other words, governments spend money on
large weapons programs – and have many hired

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:19.330
soldiers…

00:05:19.330 --> 00:05:23.240
But all that doesn’t matter much on a day-to-day
basis.

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:26.860
That’s how we end up with news like this:

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.979
(“The Legion declares an all-out war on
obesity.

00:05:30.979 --> 00:05:35.810
The elite unit of the Spanish infantry launches
a forced plan to combat obesity among its

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:36.840
members.”

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:37.840
ElPaís)

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:40.590
But, honestly, wait a moment... because that
isn’t the worst of it.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:45.020
Perhaps what annoys the US officers the most
are the rules with which some of the European

00:05:45.020 --> 00:05:49.199
armies have entered combat under NATO, for
example in Afghanistan.

00:05:49.199 --> 00:05:53.220
See, many European governments don’t want
to assume any kind of risk, so they order

00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.940
their military commanders to maintain their
soldiers away from the front.

00:05:56.940 --> 00:06:01.250
In the case of the Afghan campaign, for example,
US soldiers sharing testimonies in which they

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:05.810
were denied medical evacuation by Europeans
because they were in a combat zone weren’t

00:06:05.810 --> 00:06:06.810
strange.

00:06:06.810 --> 00:06:08.280
Listen up.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:10.169
(“Afghanistan year 2008.

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:14.230
We were returning from a mission when a rocket
hit the vehicle that was in front of mine.

00:06:14.230 --> 00:06:17.979
Our Humvee was suddenly being attacked by
machine gun fire.

00:06:17.979 --> 00:06:22.159
Of the 11 vehicles that entered the ambush
area, four were left inoperative and a fifth

00:06:22.159 --> 00:06:23.159
one was destroyed.

00:06:23.159 --> 00:06:26.550
One of our boys had died and a dozen were
wounded

00:06:26.550 --> 00:06:30.550
We were six hours away by car from our base,
it was getting dark and the Taliban were still

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:31.550
attacking us.

00:06:31.550 --> 00:06:35.610
When we called to ask for a medical evacuation,
they asked us if the landing area was still

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:36.610
hot.

00:06:36.610 --> 00:06:37.889
We said yes, we were being attacked.

00:06:37.889 --> 00:06:41.750
At that moment a voice with a foreign accent
told me that their rules prevented them from

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:43.379
providing us assistance.

00:06:43.379 --> 00:06:47.020
He asked me if we could secure the landing
area or if we could move.

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.880
Minutes turned into hours and the Taliban
continued attacking us.

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:53.740
Then, out of nowhere, a voice came up on the
radio, it was American.

00:06:53.740 --> 00:06:58.240
A couple of Blackhawk helicopters, from a
totally different mission, had heard our help

00:06:58.240 --> 00:06:59.240
call.

00:06:59.240 --> 00:07:00.659
The pilots deviated immediately.

00:07:00.659 --> 00:07:04.229
Within 15 minutes they landed and our wounded
were evacuated.

00:07:04.229 --> 00:07:08.250
Our superiors told us that the NATO country
who was responsible for medical evacuations

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:12.580
had a rule that prevented them from intervening
in still hot areas.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:21.669
We were furious”.

00:07:21.669 --> 00:07:24.130
Just a second, this is very important.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:28.340
In Afghanistan alone, nearly 900 European
soldiers have lost their lives.

00:07:28.340 --> 00:07:32.300
We’re talking about political orders that
don’t happen in all cases, but which are

00:07:32.300 --> 00:07:33.300
very common.

00:07:33.300 --> 00:07:36.050
And, no, it's not a matter of money... but
of commitment.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:45.599
Every year, the European NATO countries spend
almost 300 billion dollars, that is, together

00:07:45.599 --> 00:07:56.000
they have the second largest military budget
after the United States.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:00.651
In spite of this, in Washington they want
European NATO countries to substantially increase

00:08:00.651 --> 00:08:08.280
their military spending... and that’s where
the magical 2% of the GDP figure appears.

00:08:08.280 --> 00:08:18.120
(THE 2% MYTH)

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:21.580
As he made very clear at the last NATO summit,
Donald Trump is furious.

00:08:21.580 --> 00:08:25.539
According to the vast majority of the countries
of the Atlantic Alliance, they’re late in

00:08:25.539 --> 00:08:27.150
their quota payments.

00:08:27.150 --> 00:08:28.460
Check this out.

00:08:28.460 --> 00:08:34.170
(Many countries in NATO, which we are expected
to defend, are not only short of their current

00:08:34.170 --> 00:08:39.020
commitment of 2% (which is low), but are also
delinquent for many years in payments that

00:08:39.020 --> 00:08:40.280
have not been made.

00:08:40.280 --> 00:08:42.540
Will they reimburse the U.S.?. Donald Trump)

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:47.880
Well... one of two is true: either he’s
saying this because he has no idea or he’s

00:08:47.880 --> 00:08:52.390
saying it because he wants some electoral
points… by, of course, fooling his voters.

00:08:52.390 --> 00:08:58.090
Because, let's be clear, this entire late
payments thing has no point of reference.

00:08:58.090 --> 00:09:03.590
See, 2% is a military spending objective,
from each country for its own army.

00:09:03.590 --> 00:09:06.461
It has nothing to do with NATO payments or
anything like that.

00:09:06.461 --> 00:09:12.200
That is, if a country spends less, it doesn’t
owe anything to NATO, the United States or

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:13.920
anyone else.

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:19.970
But... the fact is that in 2014, after a lot
of pressure, Barack Obama managed to get the

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:25.350
European NATO countries to commit to substantially
increasing their military budget to reach

00:09:25.350 --> 00:09:27.630
2% of their GDP by 2024.

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:31.840
And precisely due to that commitment, Europe
is the region of the world where military

00:09:31.840 --> 00:09:33.660
spending is increasing the most.

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:37.680
Check it out.

00:09:37.680 --> 00:09:43.210
Yes, you know that later Trump and especially
his supporters will say that this was all

00:09:43.210 --> 00:09:47.340
thanks to him, to his “magnificent” negotiating
skills.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:52.010
However, even if military spending increases...
it probably won’t change anything.

00:09:52.010 --> 00:09:56.920
First, this 2% figure is a random number,
which could be reached simply by raising military

00:09:56.920 --> 00:10:00.570
salaries… or cheating in all kinds of ways.

00:10:00.570 --> 00:10:06.060
Greece, for example, is one of the few countries
that complies with this and spends 2.36% of

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:10.440
its GDP... but a large part of that budget
is used to pay for military pensions.

00:10:10.440 --> 00:10:14.820
And even most of their investment in military
equipment is geared towards defense against

00:10:14.820 --> 00:10:19.960
Turkey... another NATO member country.

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:20.960
The result?

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:25.120
Even though the expenditure rule is apparently
being followed… the Greek army brings little

00:10:25.120 --> 00:10:27.730
or nothing to the organization's capabilities.

00:10:27.730 --> 00:10:31.490
But even though all of this money could be
used to really improve the armies – and

00:10:31.490 --> 00:10:35.710
of course without political commitment it
will never happen – not much will be achieved

00:10:35.710 --> 00:10:38.560
anyway.

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:42.510
European countries will continue to depend
on the United States for force deployment…

00:10:42.510 --> 00:10:46.200
because each country by its own wouldn’t
be able to afford to maintain that kind of

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:49.370
global deployment, intelligence and supply
capabilities.

00:10:49.370 --> 00:10:53.580
And, of course, given that in Europe military
operations aren’t popular, neither the political

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:57.440
commitment nor the rules for entering into
combat will change.

00:10:57.440 --> 00:11:02.990
That is, we would have larger armies but at
the moment of truth, very similar deficiencies.

00:11:02.990 --> 00:11:04.940
But also, let’s see.

00:11:04.940 --> 00:11:08.360
The armed forces can perform four essential
functions:

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:10.720
Deterrence;
Intervention and deployment;

00:11:10.720 --> 00:11:12.620
occupation
and 4) cybersecurity

00:11:12.620 --> 00:11:19.960
For none of these tasks is the current European
countries budget a hindrance.

00:11:19.960 --> 00:11:27.530
To fight terrorism or quickly deploy somewhere
in the world and face a special situation,

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:32.160
you don’t really need multiple aircraft
carriers, that is, redundant forces.

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:39.020
It’s much more important to be able to deploy
quickly and manage a conflict.

00:11:39.020 --> 00:11:43.350
For example, there’s the latest United States
and NATO wars: Afghanistan, Iraq and Libya.

00:11:43.350 --> 00:11:49.790
In all of them, their military victory was
simple... but the postwar period... was an

00:11:49.790 --> 00:11:51.370
absolute disaster.

00:11:51.370 --> 00:11:55.350
Preparing forces to manage local conflicts,
and using the different countries’ institutions

00:11:55.350 --> 00:11:59.700
to train the national forces--not only the
military, but also police forces and trial

00:11:59.700 --> 00:12:05.780
courts, prisons and power plants, etc. would
probably have been a lot more useful.

00:12:05.780 --> 00:12:10.010
At the end of the day, dear viewers, facing
a conflict requires a lot of things... and

00:12:10.010 --> 00:12:11.540
not always the same ones.

00:12:11.540 --> 00:12:16.190
The threats we face today don’t require
aircraft carriers or fighter-bombers, but

00:12:16.190 --> 00:12:17.370
agility.

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:21.230
Europe doesn’t need to spend more, it spends
a lot, it needs to spend better.

00:12:21.230 --> 00:12:26.350
And that's where the idea of the European
army emerged.

00:12:26.350 --> 00:12:34.130
(PESCO, A EUROPEAN ARMY?)

00:12:34.130 --> 00:12:40.540
In December 2017, the European Union launched
the PESCO program, the Permanent Structured

00:12:40.540 --> 00:12:41.540
Cooperation.

00:12:41.540 --> 00:12:42.660
Its objective?

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:46.610
Coordinating the countries that participate
in the program in terms of security, acquisition

00:12:46.610 --> 00:12:49.510
of war materials and joint defense and peace
operations.

00:12:49.510 --> 00:12:53.300
In other words, to begin integrating the various
armed forces.

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:57.560
The program is still in a very incipient phase
and isn’t considered the equivalent of a

00:12:57.560 --> 00:12:58.660
European army.

00:12:58.660 --> 00:13:04.360
However... this could be the best solution
to all their problems.

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:09.320
The 25 countries that make up the PESCO program,
by integrating their armies, their weapons

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:13.600
purchases, their training and even by sharing
resources could mean that Europe, even while

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:21.800
spending less, would have a modern, well-equipped
army with great deployment capabilities.

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:26.430
After all, if a country like Spain spends
a fortune buying Leopard 2 tanks, which has

00:13:26.430 --> 00:13:29.850
happened, and in practice – due to a lack
of means to deploy them – only uses them

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:35.510
to defend against Portugal, they’ve made
a totally unnecessary expense, don’t you

00:13:35.510 --> 00:13:36.510
think?

00:13:36.510 --> 00:13:40.630
Folks, today the risks and challenges are
global, so I sincerely believe that Europe

00:13:40.630 --> 00:13:45.380
needs more integration, to do things better
and seek to complete the US’s military contributions

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:49.670
within the NATO framework… but not by spending
more.

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:55.070
Almost 300 billion dollars a year should be
more than enough.

00:13:55.070 --> 00:13:59.170
But now it's your turn, do you think Europe
should develop its own army?

00:13:59.170 --> 00:14:01.550
Leave your answer in the comments as well
as in the survey.

